// @test: typecheck-pass
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: modules, mount, name-resolution, stdlib, newtype
//
// Pin (T1002) — a module's surface must list the types that module
// declares, whatever syntax declared them.
//
// `core/security/tuf/types.vr` declares seventeen public types. Before
// this fix the compiler answered:
//
//     mount security.tuf.types.{KeyId};
//     error<E401>: cannot find `KeyId` in module `core.security.tuf.types`
//       note: module `core.security.tuf.types` exports 24 item(s);
//             showing 10: DelegatedRole, Delegations, MetafileSpec,
//             RoleDef, RoleName, RootMetadata, RootRoles, Signature, …
//
// The note contradicts the source. `KeyId` is declared on line 146 of the
// very file whose surface is being listed, and its siblings on lines 130,
// 155, 166, 176 are all present. One file, one `public type` each, and one
// of them is not in its own module.
//
// The cause is not in the module system. The archive writer attributes a
// type to the module its DESCRIPTOR names, and falls back to the archive
// ENTRY path when the descriptor names nobody:
//
//     ty.origin_module … .unwrap_or_else(|| module_path.clone())
//
// Four of codegen's type-declaration arms — `Unit`, `Newtype`,
// single-element `Tuple`, `Quotient` — built their descriptor through the
// struct-update default, where `origin_module` is `None`. `Record`,
// `Sum` and `Alias` stamped it. So the DECLARATION FORM decided whether
// the compiler recorded who declared the type: a nested file's surface
// kept its records and sums and lost its newtypes and unit types. In
// `core/compress/mod_gzip.vr` that meant three of its names — `Gzip`,
// `Deflate`, `Zlib`, all `is ()` — leaving a surface of one.
//
// The form that hurt most is the least suspicious one: `type KeyId is
// (Text);` — the ordinary parenthesised newtype — parses as a ONE-ELEMENT
// TUPLE and lowers through the `Tuple` arm, not the `Newtype` arm. That
// is why the first repair, which stamped `Unit`, moved three named cases
// and none of the ninety measured ones.
//
// Radius, measured on one binary over 90 single-line newtypes in `core/`:
// 18 could not be mounted by the path their own source states; 72 could.
// The fallback usually lands right — when a file IS its module's entry,
// the entry path and the declaring path are the same string. Only a
// nested file that a parent re-exports diverges. The 72 are the control:
// a WRONG declaring module would repair the 18 and break them.
//
// The carry this depends on is v2.12 / T0555, which taught the archive to
// prefer the declaring FILE over the directory entry. It built the
// channel; nothing checked that every declaration arm fills it.
//
// Why it stayed invisible: the same mount written `core.security.tuf.
// types.{KeyId}` resolved all along — that spelling reaches a different
// surface (61 items, and `KeyId` is in it) and never consults the one
// this file is about. That divergence is its own defect, T1005; this
// spec pins the bare spelling, which is the one `core/` and the docs use.

mount security.tuf.types.{KeyId};

// The `is ()` form, from a different arm and a different subtree. It is
// here because the first repair stamped ONLY this arm: it moved this
// case and two siblings (`compress.mod_gzip.{Gzip}`,
// `tracing.sampler.{AlwaysOn}`) while moving none of the ninety
// parenthesised newtypes. One form green is not the rule holding — that
// is precisely the reading that sent the first fix out half-done.
mount tracing.id.{DefaultIdGenerator};

// The control, and the reason this file is not merely "a mount works":
// a sibling declared in the SAME file, in a form that was stamped all
// along, resolved before this fix and must keep resolving. If it ever
// goes red, the declaring module now being stamped is the WRONG one, and
// the mount above proves nothing.
mount security.tuf.types.{RoleName};

fn main() {
    print("ok");
}
